<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="220766a2">&lt;h4&gt;
    Change Request
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    When an internal or external request is made for a significant change to the original requirements as documented in a
    project charter, a change request form is filled out. The scope change template is designed to keep a formal record of
    all change requests during the project. Although the document title only references scope, the template has broader
    application and documents change impact on work products, cost, schedule, resources, benefits and/or stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Use this template to document project change requests unless the client has its own template. Both EY and the client
    should use the same form to document changes. If there is a significant change, an addendum may be required for the
    engagement agreement and should be issued along with the project change request.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Upon considering the change request management/the steering committee may elect to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Approve the change
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Request more information
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Reject the change
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        If the change is accepted the project plan and associated budget/timeline/task list should be adjusted accordingly
        and noted in the Project Change Log.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    PCR examples are as follows:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Overall project schedule is going to slip and an extension to the schedule is needed
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Overall project costs are going to exceed the project’s budget
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more project deliverables need to be added/removed from the project
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more milestones need to have its target completion date adjusted.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more additional resources are required to complete the project (generally this equates to increased project
        costs).
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Change Log
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Use the change log to facilitate the process for managing changes in the budget, resource, schedule, work products and
    milestones. The log is complementary to the scope change template, which provides the analysis and approval to
    authorize changes to the project. The change log provides a template to record changes that impact the project.
    Although the document title only references scope, the template has broader application and includes changes to budget,
    resource, schedule, work products and milestones in its list of change categories
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Change Control
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Change control is an expected and integral part of the iterative discovery and implementation process. The process
    constructively manages change and uses a Change Control Board (CCB) to review and determine the status of PCRs. Good
    change control allows for continuous, real-time adjustments to&amp;nbsp;provide&amp;nbsp;the relevant and accurate&amp;nbsp;final
    deliverables. The team should make sure the positive change results outweigh the original cost of making the change.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Controlling change is a critical success factor, as it is fundamental to delivering the first two of the three key
    elements of EY Service Quality: 1) Meeting the requirements of all stakeholders, in accordance with professional and EY
    standards; 2) Delivering superior service to clients; and 3) Delivering services consistently across time, geographies
    and service lines. Decisions made during a project, whether approvals or denials, are important to retain in the
    project&apos;s history. The templates provides a standard approach for the analysis of project changes along with the
    supporting justification for same.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Managing scope is one of the most important factors within a project, and formalizing the process helps keep the
    project on track. Note that all scope change requests may not be approved by the client, but the process of identifying
    scope changes and their potential impact if done / not done is helpful in maintaining project governance.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
